The general phenomenon is known: reflection words alone are not the cause of the accuracy (s1's forced 'Wait' gains at most 1 point on MATH500; untargeted or random steering does not recover the RL gap in 2510.07364; excess reflection hurts in 2506.12217 and SEAL). What the notes do not show anywhere is this specific measurement:
(a) a cued, untrained base steered until its written rate of hold-on words equals the RL-Zero model's (8.32 vs 8.81 per 1,000 tokens), with MATH-500 unchanged (413 vs 410.5 of 500, RL 457);
(b) the three controls the closest paper (2506.12217) lacks: an equal-norm random direction (409), a logit bias on the six tokens (421, not below the activation push), and a measured rate under steering;
(c) a blind reading of what the inserted Waits do (16 of 20 filler for the push vs 7 of 20 corrections for the model's own), plus the count on the 65 RL-only problems (no gain: 35 vs 42 and 37 unsteered);
(d) a logit-lens-style (J-lens) readout direction rather than a difference-of-means vector.
It also contradicts the +12-point claim of 2506.12217 in a different setting (cued base with a strong baseline of 82 % vs their 45 % instruct baseline), which is worth stating as a setting difference, not a refutation. Limits on the novelty claim: one sample per arm, constant-position push only (2510.07364 suggests a gated push is what helps, which was not scored here), and the literature check could not include a live search.
